sjdevnull@yahoo.com wrote:
> Pete Ley <pete....@hotmail.com> wrote:
>
>> I'm still pretty new to Nethack. I started playing about 6 months ago but
>> stopped and have recently picked it back up again. I've noticed that I
>> always lose my starting pet about Dlvl 3 or 4. I'm not very good at
>> keeping him around. I never really rely on him to kill anything, just
>> pretty much let him do his thing. I try to get him to be adjacent when I
>> descend but if it's taking too long I just give up. Or he dies.
>>
>> Well this time I let the RNG pick me a healer and I figure a good
>> strategy is to let him do a lot of the work and just work on keeping him
>> alive. How can I go about keeping him near me and such? Is there any art
>> or skill to it? Any tips you can give me, rgrn?
>
> 1) Use a magic whistle to teleport the pet near you

Sometimes one of the shops in mine town have one.  They are rare in the
early game.  A magic whistle is so useful that even if I don't currently
have any pets I will keep one in the bag for easy access in case I tame
a pet.

> 2) Use a non-magic whistle to cause the pet to move toward you for a
> few turns

The mine town watch carry regular whistles.  On the way down some pets
(large dogs) are strong enough to attack watchmen. Some pets (horses)
are even strong enough to attack the watch captain or the priest.  If I
see my pet attacking either the watch captain or priest I try to close
him in a room while I explore.  In the end having a non-magic whistle is
extremely common once you come back up from mines end.  So is the 50-50
long sword or silver saber carried by the watch captain.  I've lost
plenty of pets to the watch captain's weapon.

The problem with using a regular whistle - It wakes up any nymph on the
level.  Telepathy and a blindfold or towel is a must.  Waking up nymphs
is a very common cause of early deaths.

> 3) Keep a tripe ration in main inventory (not in a bag/sack) to cause
> the pet to try to stick near you

If I try to be ahead of my pet in corridors while exploring I risk
meeting a crowd of hostiles but I have a chance to run to any food items
on the floor.

> 4) Displace the pet so that it's in front of you in corridors and the
> like, and let it run ahead of you

If I try to be behind my pet in corridors while exploring the pet will
rush to any tripe rations and get them before I have a chance.

But having your pet explore ahead of you is a very good way to keep
track of your pet.

> 5) When a monster's around, keep the pet between you and the monster
> (keep moving behind it on your turn)

In mine town if I want my pet to take out a monster I dance around the
monster.  It gives my pet a lot of time to approach the monster and
fight it.  I do that a lot with peacefuls when I suspect I want their
stuff.  Peaceful hobbits and dwarves have good early game items.

> 6) How tough a monster is doesn't matter to how much it helps the pet
> level up, so definitely let the pet kill the weaklings

This is a very useful point.  It tempting to use dancing to have a pet
fight the dangerous monsters and take out the popcorn monsters myself. 
For pet survival it works the other way around though.  It's a balance
that flips back and forth as the game progresses which level of monsters
you want your pet to fight.

Pets will attack a monster based on its level not on the monster's
items.  I lose a lot of pets in minetown the the weapons carried by the
watch and some early pets to dwarves with mattocks.  So I need to ask
myself my I want my pet to fight any one specific monster.  Is it a
popcorn monster for pet advancement?  Is it a peaceful monster and I
want it's likely items?  Is it a monster with a weapon likely to take
out my pet before it decides to retreat?  Is it a situation of the pet
or me dying at the moment?

> 7) As a healer, use your stethoscope freely and heal the pet when it's
> injured

A bit simpler for other character classes - A pet will attack monsters
up to a level or two above the pet's current level.  Monsters only hit
pets after a pet takes the first swing so the first blow is the pet's
advantage in combat.  A pet will continue the combat as long as the pet
has (half?  about half? some other percentage?) of its hit points and
then the pet will stop fighting and retreat.  Monsters pursue you not
your pet.

If your pet stops combat he's probably injured enough that he needs time
to heal so maneuver him away from crowds of monsters not towards them. 
I have lost pets because I put them into a crowd of watchmen and the
watchmen took enough swings to move my pet from mostly healthy to dead
very qucikly.

8) Take your time exploring so you keep track of your pet.  If you go
down a corridor and your pet does not follow try again to try to get him
to follow.  If your pet seems more interested in a different direction
consider exploring that diriction instead.  Ignoring a pet is how they
wander off to a different part of the dungeon.  Do that and your pet
will fight monsters, find traps, maybe even die without you learning
anything from it.


